          "The dates to keep in mind: Two points in this timeline really deserve a closer look. First, the obligation to receive e-invoices applies to everyone from September 1, 2026, small businesses included.\nJust remember one thing: a PDF forwarded by email will no longer cut it for a transaction between French businesses. The reform rolls out in two stages, based on company size. The dates to keep in mind: Two points in this timeline really deserve a closer look. First, the obligation to receive e-invoices applies to everyone from September 1, 2026, small businesses included.\nIt's not an invitation to wait until the last minute, but it does take the pressure off a big-bang switchover on September 1, 2026. The reform covers every business, freelancer, and independent professional subject to VAT in France, regardless of size, revenue, legal structure, or tax regime. As soon as you invoice other French businesses (domestic B2B transactions), you're in scope.\nIn two stages. On September 1, 2026, every business must be able to receive e-invoices, and large companies and mid-sized companies (ETIs) must already issue them.\nYou may well have until 2027 to issue your own invoices in electronic format, but your large-company and ETI suppliers will start sending you theirs from September 2026. By that date, you'll already need to know how to receive and process them. Second, small businesses have until September 1, 2027 to issue their invoices in electronic format and to transmit their transaction data to the tax authority.",
