        mime_type: 'text/html',
        filename: null,
        title: 'French e-invoicing mandate: your complete 2026 compliance guide',
        snippet:
          'That means even smaller businesses can’t wait until 2027 to start preparing. Your systems and platform need to be capable of receiving compliant e-invoices from 1 September 2026.\nFrance’s e-invoicing and e-reporting mandate starts rolling out on 1 September 2026. It’s a significant reform — and not just because it changes how invoices are sent. It changes how they’re created, exchanged, validated, and reported to the French tax authority. It’s possible that if you’re operating in France, you’ll have more gaps in your readiness than you realise. Let’s go through what the mandate requires, how the platform ecosystem works, which deadlines apply to your business, and what you need to do to be ready.\nThe receiving obligation arrives first — and it applies to every business, regardless of size. That means even smaller businesses can’t wait until 2027 to start preparing. Your systems and platform need to be capable of receiving compliant e-invoices from 1 September 2026.\nThe phased rollout starts 1 September 2026. All businesses must be able to receive e-invoices from that date. Obligations for issuing e-invoices and meeting e-reporting requirements depend on business size. Large enterprises and ETIs must comply from September 2026.\nFrance’s e-invoicing mandate requires VAT-taxable businesses established in France to exchange structured electronic invoices for in-scope domestic B2B transactions. A compliant e-invoice is machine-readable structured data — not a scanned PDF or an emailed invoice. The rollout begins 1 September 2026.',
