        public_url: 'https://complyance.io/blog/france-e-invoicing-key-dates-deadlines',
        mime_type: 'text/html',
        filename: null,
        title: 'France E-Invoicing: Key Dates and Deadlines',
        snippet:
          '## France E-Invoicing Timeline  \n| Date | Businesses concerned | Main requirement |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 September 2026 | All businesses concerned by the reform, including businesses that only need to receive supplier e-invoices | Be able to receive electronic invoices |\n| 1 September 2026 | Large enterprises and ETIs | Issue electronic invoices and meet applicable e-reporting requirements |\n| No later than 1 September 2027 | SMEs and micro-enterprises | Issue electronic invoices and meet applicable e-reporting requirements |  \nDate Businesses concerned Main requirement 1 September 2026 All businesses concerned by the reform, including businesses that only need to receive supplier e-invoices Be able to receive elec  \nThe receiving requirement is therefore earlier for everyone concerned, while the issuing requirement depends on business size.\n## What Should Businesses Prepare Before 2026?  \nThe first step is to understand the business’s scope.  \nA useful preparation checklist includes:  \n1. **Choose a PA or compatible solution connected to a PA.**\n2. **Map incoming and outgoing invoice flows.**\n3. **Confirm ERP or accounting-software connectivity.**\n4. **Classify customers as French businesses, consumers, or foreign operators.**\n5. **Identify e-reporting requirements for relevant transactions.**\n6. **Check invoice data and customer master data.**\n7. **Test receiving and issuing processes before the applicable deadline.**  \nBusinesses should also confirm their official size classification. The applicable category should be assessed using the relevant legal-entity and SIREN-level information where required, rather than relying only on an internal description such as “small company.”\n## Does Company Size Determine Whether a Transaction Is In Scope?  \nNo.  \nCompany size mainly affects the implementation date for issuing electronic invoices.  \nWhether a transaction is subject to domestic B2B e-invoicing depends on factors such as VAT status, establishment, transaction type, and French VAT and invoicing rules.  \nA small business can therefore have an in-scope transaction even though its issuance deadline is later than that of a large enterprise.  \nThis is an important distinction when reviewing **France e-invoicing requirements**.',
